 \end{equation}
-A training row receives one fold-specific prediction per family. A test row receives a within-family average over five fits.
-
-TinyLinear computes logits $\ell_i = W z_i + b$ with $W \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 12}$ and no hidden layer. It is trained with weighted cross-entropy. That objective is not macro-F1, yet macro-F1 is what selects the checkpoint, and every epoch is assessed on the same fold later included in reported validation performance.
-
-Final test labels are
-\begin{equation}
-\hat{y}(x) = \arg\max_c \frac{1}{5}\sum_{f=1}^{5} q_f(\bar{z}(x))_c
-\end{equation}
-over the selected heads $q_f$. Five softmax outputs are averaged. In general, this is not equivalent to applying one head obtained by averaging coefficients. The construction implies 39 scalar weights and biases per head and 195 across five heads (A), before any upstream state is counted.
-
-Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} summarises the computation. It is not fully nested. When $V_f$ serves as a meta validation set, a meta training row drawn from another $V_g$ was represented by a base fit that used labels from $V_f$.
-
+LightGBM additionally receives the numerical features, suppressed in this notation for readability. The notation excludes a fold from its base estimator fit, not from global preprocessing, class weights, or LightGBM stopping selection.
+
+Three Meta-LR fits are trained from the single global OOF feature matrix using the same split scheme. Their test probabilities are averaged. The selected final predictor combines six averaged base outputs and the averaged Meta-LR output, so Meta-LR is an active component rather than merely an alternative candidate. If $F_j$ is candidate $j$'s pooled OOF macro-F1 and $p_j(x)$ its test probability vector, then
+\begin{equation}
+\alpha_j=\frac{F_j^2}{\sum_{k=1}^{7}F_k^2},\qquad
+\hat y(x)=\arg\max_c\sum_{j=1}^{7}\alpha_j p_j(x)_c.
+\label{eq:ensemble}
+\end{equation}
+The weights are learned from labeled evaluation outcomes. ``Weighted'' therefore does not mean a prespecified, label-independent average. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} separates this prediction path from the independent profiling operation.
 
